<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-norm-equivalence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Norm equivalence
  </title>
  
  <idx>
    norm equivalence
  </idx>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Two norms <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } , \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } \colon V \to \R </m> on a real or complex vector space <m> V </m> are called <term> equivalent </term> if there are positive real numbers <m> A , B \in \opint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m> so that
      
      <md>
        A \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 1 } \leq B \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 }
      </md>
      
      for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that norm equivalence is an equivalence relation on the set of norms on <m> V </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        First let <m> \norm{ \oparg } \colon V \to \R </m> be a norm on <m> V </m>, and note that
        
        <md>
          1 \norm{ \vect{ v } } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } } \leq 1 \norm{ \vect{ v } }
        </md>
        
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>. Thus <m> \norm{ \oparg } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg } </m> are equivalent, and so norm equivalence is reflexive.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Now let <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } , \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } \colon V \to \R </m> be norms on <m> V </m>, and suppose that <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } </m> are equivalent. Then there are positive real numbers <m> A , B \in \opint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          A \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 1 } \leq B \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 }
        </md>
      
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \frac{ 1 }{ B } \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 1 } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq \frac{ 1 }{ A } \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 1 }
        </md>
        
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>. Thus <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } </m> are equivalent, and so norm equivalence is symmetric.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Finally, let <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } , \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } , \norm{ \oparg }_{ 3 } \colon V \to \R </m> be norms on <m> V </m>, and suppose that <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } </m> are equivalent and that <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 2 } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 3 } </m> are equivalent. Then there are positive real numbers <m> A , B , C , D \in \opint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m> so that
        
        <md>
          A \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 1 } \leq B \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 }
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          C \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 3 } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq D \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 3 }
        </md>
      
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            A C \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 3 } \amp \leq A \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 1 }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \leq B \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 2 } \leq B D \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ 3 }
          </mrow>
        </md>
        
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>. Thus <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 1 } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ 3 } </m> are equivalent, and so norm equivalence is transitive.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Since norm equivalence is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation on the set of norms on <m> V </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that for a given natural number <m> n \in \N </m>, any two <m> p </m>-norms <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ p } </m> on <m> n </m>-dimensional real coordinate space <m> \R^{ n } </m> for <m> 1 \leq p \leq \infty </m> are all equivalent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We will show that any <m> p </m>-norm on <m> \R^{ n } </m> with <m> 1 \leq p \lt \infty </m> is equivalent to the <m> \infty </m>-norm; (a) above implies that this suffices. To that end, let <m> \vect{ v } = \of{ v_{ k } }_{ k = 1 }^{ n } \in \R^{ n } </m>, and first note that
        
        <md>
          \abs{ v_{ j } } = \of{ \abs{ v_{ j } }^{ p } }^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } } \leq \of{ \sum_{ k = 1 }^{ m }{ \abs{ v_{ k } }^{ p } } }^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } } = \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ p }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> 1 \leq j \leq m </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ \infty } = \max_{ 1 \leq j \leq m }{ \abs{ v_{ j } } } \leq \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ p }
        </md>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        On the other hand, we note that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ p } \amp = \of{ \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ \abs{ v_{ j } }^{ p } } }^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } } \leq \of{ \sum_{ j = 1 }^{ m }{ \max_{ 1 \leq k \leq m }{ \abs{ v_{ k } }^{ p } } } }^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ m \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ \infty }^{ p } }^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } } = m^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } } \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ \infty }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Since
        
        <md>
          1 \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ \infty } \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ p } \leq m^{ \frac{ 1 }{ p } } \norm{ \vect{ v } }_{ \infty }
        </md>
        
        for all vectors <m> \vect{ v } \in V </m>, <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ p } </m> and <m> \norm{ \oparg }_{ \infty } </m> are equivalent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
